I"�-<p>Our research began with a question about <strong>computational concept formation</strong>: can an AI identify a recurring computation, assign it a symbol, and compose it with subsequent discoveries? Repeated addition provides an elementary model:</p>

\[f(n,x)=\underbrace{x+\cdots+x}_{n\text{ terms}}
\quad\longrightarrow\quad
\sigma(n,x):=f(n,x).\]

<p>Here, $n$ is a positive integer, $x$ is the value being added, and $\sigma$ names the procedure $f$. If $\sigma$ is assigned a dedicated input token, let $\mathbf e_\sigma\in\mathbb R^{d_{\mathrm{model}}}$ denote that token’s <strong>input embedding vector</strong>, where $d_{\mathrm{model}}$ is the model’s embedding dimension. This vector lets the model receive the symbol as input; it does not itself guarantee that the model can execute $f$. An output vocabulary entry may require a separate output vector.</p>

<p>The association $\sigma\leftrightarrow f\leftrightarrow\mathbf e_\sigma$ is our motivating proposal, not a mechanism jointly demonstrated by the six papers. They address distinct parts of program induction, vocabulary expansion, tool creation, and emergent communication.</p>

